#x60;lavaflow.repository_ci&#x60; | Normal repository CI availability specifically. Eligible fixes use it only after the owner grants &#x60;allow_ungated_workflows&#x60; for the connected repository and installation. The repository controls its CI and credential access. This capability does not authorize execution or prove a workflow ran | | &#x60;lavaflow.code_quality&#x60; | LavaFlow Code Quality, which reviews the code each frontend production deploy changes. Here it reports only whether Code Quality is released in this environment; whether a project has it comes from &#x60;GET /projects/{id}/capabilities&#x60; | | &#x60;lavaflow.slack&#x60; | Reporting problems to LavaFlow from Slack: installing the Volcano Slack app, connecting Slack identities, and binding channels to projects. Requires LavaFlow availability and a configured Slack app | | &#x60;lavaflow.aikido&#x60; | LavaFlow fixing the security issues Aikido finds: connecting an Aikido workspace and binding projects to it. Requires LavaFlow availability | | &#x60;functions.response_streaming&#x60; | Streaming an HTTP-mode function&#39;s response on its own URL as the function writes it | | &#x60;frontend_routes&#x60; | Creating and changing Frontend routes; listing, deleting, and serving existing ones work either way | | &#x60;frontends.candidates&#x60; | Creating, redeploying, promoting and rolling back to a frontend Candidate; reading, deleting and rolling back to the frontend&#39;s own runtime always work | | &#x60;databases.migrations&#x60; | Starting migration runs and recording a baseline; reading history and runs works either way | | &#x60;shares&#x60; | Creating, switching on and publishing shares, and forking one | | &#x60;ai_gateway&#x60; | The Volcano AI gateway: creating a key that holds &#x60;ai.invoke&#x60; with &#x60;POST /projects/{id}/service-keys&#x60;, and minting the first run token of a run; reading, regenerating and deleting existing keys, and reading, extending, revoking and minting again for a run already minted for, work either way | | &#x60;realtime.http_publish&#x60; | Publishing a message with &#x60;POST /realtime/v1/publish&#x60;; while Realtime is paused it stays available, and the route answers &#x60;503&#x60; &#x60;feature_paused&#x60; |  A capability missing from the list is unavailable, and a capability under another, such as &#x60;sandboxes.sessions&#x60;, is unavailable whenever its parent is. While a capability is unavailable, requests that start new work answer &#x60;404&#x60; or &#x60;400&#x60; with code &#x60;feature_unavailable&#x60;; refresh this list when you receive that code. Reading, deleting, and terminating what already exists keep working unless the whole feature is absent from the environment. Every caller in an environment gets the same answer; &#x60;GET /projects/{id}/capabilities&#x60; answers for one project.  &#x60;functions.response_streaming&#x60; refuses nothing: while it is unavailable, a function&#39;s own URL still answers and sends a streamed response whole, once the function finishes, up to 6 MiB with headers; a larger one fails the invocation. Frontend Function routes stream either way.  After these, the list names each first-party app Volcano runs in this environment, such as &#x60;builder&#x60;, by its own ID. Such an app is available while it accepts new work. When only the app&#39;s own release is off, work it already accepted continues; when first-party app mounts are off, its mount refuses every request with 404 &#x60;feature_unavailable&#x60;. 
